## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement EDF scheduling, bounded queues, per-producer fairness, backpressure, and safe shutdown under load.

## Key Concepts
- Deadline-aware scheduling (EDF)
- Bounded queues with per-producer rate limits
- Backpressure signaling and cancellation
- Graceful shutdown with in-flight tracking
- Correctness under contention and timing jitter

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal EDF scheduler (Node.js)
class Task {
  constructor(id, deadline, payload){ this.id=id; this.deadline=deadline; this.payload=payload; this.cancelled=false; }
}
class EDFScheduler {
  constructor(concurrency=2){ this.queue=[]; this.concurrency=concurrency; this.active=0; this.running=true; }
  enqueue(t){ this.queue.push(t); this.queue.sort((a,b)=>a.deadline-b.deadline); }
  async nextTask(){ while(this.running){ if(this.queue.length){ const t=this.queue.shift(); if(!t.cancelled) return t; continue; } await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1)); } return null; }
  async startWorker(){ if(this.active>=this.concurrency) return; this.active++; (async ()=>{ while(this.running){ const t=await this.nextTask(); if(!t) break; await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,10)); console.log(`done ${t.id}`); } this.active--; })(); }
  shutdown(){ this.running=false; }
}
(async ()=>{ const s=new EDFScheduler(2); const now=Date.now(); s.enqueue(new Task(1, now+200, {})); s.enqueue(new Task(2, now+100, {})); s.enqueue(new Task(3, now+150, {})); s.startWorker(); s.startWorker(); setTimeout(()=>s.shutdown(),500);})();
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's practical understanding of per-source ordering with shared throughput, backpressure mechanisms, and graceful degradation in a realistic chat notification system.

## Key Concepts

- **Per-room FIFO buffers**: Ensure events within each room maintain insertion order
- **Global bounded worker queue**: Limit total memory usage while enabling cross-room parallelism
- **Backpressure thresholds**: Prevent producers from overwhelming the system
- **Degradation via event coalescing**: Reduce load by merging rapid state changes (typing indicators) into single events
- **Graceful shutdown**: Drain in-flight work before terminating without data loss

## Code Example

```javascript
class RoomBuffer {
  constructor(maxBacklog = 1000) {
    this.queues = new Map();
    this.maxBacklog = maxBacklog;
  }

  enqueue(room, event) {
    if (!this.queues.has(room)) {
      this.queues.set(room, []);
    }
    const queue = this.queues.get(room);
    if (queue.length >= this.maxBacklog) {
      if (event.type === 'typing') {
        return this.coalesceTyping(room, event);
      }
      return false; // backpressure signal
    }
    queue.push({ ...event, seq: this.getNextSeq(room) });
    return true;
  }

  coalesceTyping(room, event) {
    const queue = this.queues.get(room);
    const last = queue[queue.length - 1];
    if (last?.type === 'typing') {
      last.count = (last.count || 1) + 1;
      return true;
    }
    queue.push({ ...event, count: 1 });
    return true;
  }
}
```

This implementation provides per-room ordering via sequence numbers, backpressure via bounded queue depth, and automatic coalescing of typing events during high load.

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design practical concurrency patterns beyond simple per-key in-order pipelines. It introduces a dynamic shard rebalancer and an explicit overflow policy, reflecting real systems where tenants differ in throughput and backpressure must propagate upstream.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant buffers in a bounded shared queue
- Epoch-based commits to preserve order
- Dynamic shard rebalancer for load balancing across workers
- Overflow policy choices with fairness and backpressure
- Deterministic testing plan to verify ordering, backpressure, and shutdown

## Code Example

```javascript
class TenantBuffer {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K
    this.buffers = new Map()
  }
  enqueue(event) {
    const t = event.tenant_id
    if (!this.buffers.has(t)) this.buffers.set(t, [])
    const q = this.buffers.get(t)
    if (q.length >= this.K) return false
    q.push(event)
    return true
  }
  dequeue() {
    for (const [t, q] of this.buffers.entries()) {
      if (q.length) return q.shift()
    }
    return null
  }
}
```

